::CW_U05_L04_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft excerpt: I raised my hand to knock, then lowered it. The writer wants to emphasize hesitation in a poem. Which format choice best supports that purpose?{
~A. Put the sentence in a single paragraph with no pause.#Incorrect. This may hide the hesitation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Place "then lowered it" on a separate line to slow the action.#Correct. The line break supports pacing and meaning.
~C. Add random capital letters.#Incorrect. Random capitals distract rather than clarify. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the action from the poem.#Incorrect. Removing the action removes the moment of hesitation.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which explanation best connects a format choice to reader effect?{
~A. I used spacing because it looks neat.#Incorrect. This does not explain a craft effect.
~B. I used spacing because someone told me to.#Incorrect. This gives no reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I used spacing because all creative writing needs unusual spacing.#Incorrect. This is not a reliable rule. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I placed the final word on its own line so the reader pauses before the decision.#Correct. This names the format choice and explains the reader effect.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft: "Wait" Ellis said "I heard it too." Which prose revision best improves dialogue format?{
=A. "Wait," Ellis said. "I heard it too."#Correct. The punctuation and capitalization make the dialogue clear.
~B. wait ellis said i heard it too#Incorrect. Missing capitalization and punctuation weaken readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. WAIT ELLIS SAID I HEARD IT TOOfor this item#Incorrect. All capitals do not create correct dialogue format.
~D. "Wait" "Ellis" "said" "heard."#Incorrect. This fragments the sentence and confuses meaning.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Script context: Ellis whispers from the hallway. Which format best fits a script?{
~A. Ellis whispered from the hallway in a long paragraph.#Incorrect. This is prose narration, not script format.
~B. Someone talks somewhere.#Incorrect. This does not identify the speaker or action clearly.
=C. ELLIS (whispering from the hallway): I heard it too.#Correct. The speaker label and stage direction fit script format.
~D. A blank page with a title.#Incorrect. A title alone does not format dialogue for a script.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which format issue should a writer check in a prose story?{
~A. Whether every line break looks like a poem.#Incorrect. Prose does not need poem-style line breaks.
~B. Whether speaker labels replace all narration.#Incorrect. Speaker labels are script features, not required prose features.
~C. Whether random spacing makes the page unusual.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not guarantee quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Whether paragraph breaks and dialogue punctuation help the reader follow events and speakers.#Correct. Prose format should support clarity and readability.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q06::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A writer formats a poem as one dense paragraph even though the poem depends on pauses. What is the best revision focus?{
~A. Add character labels before every line.#Incorrect. Character labels are usually script features. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Review line breaks and stanza breaks to control pacing.#Correct. These poem format features can create purposeful pauses.
~C. Remove every image from the poem.#Incorrect. The issue is format, not necessarily imagery.
~D. Add unrelated headings.#Incorrect. Headings do not solve pacing by themselves.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q07::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which choice is an example of random decorative formatting?{
=A. Adding spaces between letters for no reason connected to meaning.#Correct. This is decoration without a clear reader effect.
~B. Starting a new paragraph when the speaker changes.#Incorrect. This supports clarity in prose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Using a stage direction to show a character's movement.#Incorrect. That can support script performance. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Placing a poem's final image on its own line to emphasize it.#Incorrect. That is a purposeful format choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q08::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A nonfiction reflection moves from childhood to the present. Which format choice can improve clarity?{
~A. Remove all paragraph breaks.#Incorrect. That can make time shifts harder to follow.
~B. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Random punctuation weakens publication quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Use a paragraph break where the time period changes.#Correct. Paragraph breaks can help readers follow shifts in time.
~D. Format it as a script without speakers.#Incorrect. A script without speakers would not fit the reflection clearly.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which checkpoint explanation is strongest?{
~A. I changed it because it looked cool.#Incorrect. This is not a craft explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I changed everything for no reason.#Incorrect. This rejects purposeful format revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. My format is better because I like it.#Incorrect. This is preference, not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I used a paragraph break before the memory shift so the reader can follow the change in time.#Correct. This names the format choice and explains how it improves clarity.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q10::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which format choice best supports a script meant to be performed?{
=A. Clear character labels and brief stage directions when movement matters.#Correct. These features help readers and performers follow the scene.
~B. One block paragraph with no speaker information.#Incorrect. That makes performance reading difficult. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Random stanza breaks.#Incorrect. Stanza breaks are poem features and may not support script performance.
~D. No indication of who is speaking.#Incorrect. Scripts require speaker clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q11::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft context: A poem ends with the word "home." The writer wants that word to feel important. Which format choice best fits?{
~A. Hide the word in the middle of a crowded line.#Incorrect. This may reduce emphasis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Put "home" on its own final line if the pause supports the poem's meaning.#Correct. The line placement can emphasize the final word purposefully.
~C. Delete the word home.#Incorrect. This removes the important word. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add ten unrelated punctuation marks after it.#Incorrect. Random punctuation distracts from meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q12::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which revision best improves publication quality in prose?{
~A. Removing all capitalization.#Incorrect. This usually weakens readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Adding random line breaks after every word.#Incorrect. That does not fit prose clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Replacing punctuation with symbols.#Incorrect. Symbols can confuse readers unless clearly purposeful.
=D. Separating a new speaker's dialogue into a new paragraph.#Correct. This helps readers track who is speaking.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q13::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which statement best defines format-specific quality work?{
~A. Making every piece look unusual.#Incorrect. Unusual formatting is not automatically high quality.
~B. Ignoring the genre because format does not matter.#Incorrect. Format affects how readers understand the work.
=C. Using the features of the chosen format to improve meaning, clarity, pacing, or readability.#Correct. Format choices should serve the reader and the piece.
~D. Choosing decorations before understanding the writing.#Incorrect. Decoration should not replace craft purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q14::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A writer chooses script format but does not label speakers. What problem will readers likely have?{
=A. The reader may not know who is speaking.#Correct. Speaker labels are essential for script clarity.
~B. The title will be too short.#Incorrect. The issue is speaker clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The poem will have too many stanzas.#Incorrect. The piece is a script, not a poem.
~D. The reader will know too much about the setting.#Incorrect. Missing speaker labels do not create that issue.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q15::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which format choice is most purposeful for a poem about a sudden interruption?{
~A. One long paragraph with no break at the interruption.#Incorrect. This may hide the sudden shift. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Break the line at the interruption so the reader experiences the stop.#Correct. The line break supports the meaning and pacing.
~C. Randomly bold every noun.#Incorrect. That does not necessarily support interruption. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the interruption from the poem.#Incorrect. That removes the central effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q16::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which response best revises a weak format explanation? Weak explanation: "I made it look better."{
~A. I made it look better because better is better.#Incorrect. This still does not explain a reader effect.
~B. I changed it because formatting is random.#Incorrect. Format should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I do not need to explain formatting.#Incorrect. The lesson requires explaining format choices. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I used shorter paragraphs in the tense scene so each action is easier to follow.#Correct. This names a format choice and explains its effect on clarity.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q17::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A prose draft has three speakers in one paragraph. Which revision focus best improves readability?{
~A. Add a poem title.#Incorrect. A title does not solve speaker tracking.
~B. Put the whole paragraph in all capitals.#Incorrect. All capitals do not clarify speakers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes and check dialogue tags.#Correct. This directly improves speaker clarity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Remove every line of dialogue.#Incorrect. Deleting all dialogue is not targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q18::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which choice best shows format supporting tone?{
=A. A quiet poem uses shorter lines and pauses to create a restrained tone.#Correct. The format choices support the intended tone.
~B. A quiet poem uses random symbols after every word.#Incorrect. Random symbols distract from tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A quiet poem ignores line breaks completely even when pauses matter.#Incorrect. Ignoring useful format tools can weaken tone.
~D. A quiet poem changes to a research report for no reason.#Incorrect. That changes the format without supporting the poem's tone.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q19::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which publication-quality check should happen before submitting a formatted piece?{
~A. Check whether every format choice is random.#Incorrect. Random choices are not the goal. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Check whether the chosen format helps the reader understand the piece.#Correct. Reader understanding is central to format-specific quality.
~C. Check whether the piece hides all meaning.#Incorrect. Publication quality should improve meaning and clarity.
~D. Check whether no one can identify the genre.#Incorrect. The format should usually make the genre clearer.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q20::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] A writer uses stage directions that are longer than the dialogue and explain every thought. What should the writer consider?{
~A. Whether to remove all speaker labels.#Incorrect. Speaker labels are still needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Whether to turn every sentence into a title.#Incorrect. That does not solve the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Whether random spacing will fix it.#Incorrect. Random spacing is not targeted revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Whether the stage directions are helping performance or slowing the scene.#Correct. Stage directions should support action and performance, not overload the script.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q21::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which format choice helps readers follow a list-like reflection?{
=A. Using clear paragraphing or section breaks when the focus shifts.#Correct. These choices help readers track organization. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Removing all breaks so every idea runs together.#Incorrect. That can reduce clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Adding unrelated stage directions.#Incorrect. Stage directions do not fit most reflections.
~D. Using line breaks randomly after every letter.#Incorrect. Random breaks harm readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q22::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which answer best explains why random formatting is a common mistake?{
~A. It always improves publication quality.#Incorrect. Random formatting often weakens quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It proves the writer understands genre.#Incorrect. Random formatting may show the opposite. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It can distract the reader because the format choice is not connected to meaning or clarity.#Correct. Format should serve the piece, not distract from it.
~D. It is required in every creative-writing format.#Incorrect. No such requirement exists. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q23::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Draft context: The writer wants a story scene to be easy to follow. Which format revision best supports clarity?{
~A. Remove every paragraph break.#Incorrect. This makes the scene harder to follow.
=B. Use paragraph breaks for shifts in speaker, action, or focus.#Correct. Paragraphing helps readers follow prose scenes. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Place all dialogue in one unpunctuated line.#Incorrect. This weakens speaker clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add random stanza breaks in the middle of sentences.#Incorrect. That does not fit prose clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q24::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which response best identifies a format mismatch?{
~A. The script has character labels and short stage directions.#Incorrect. Those features fit script format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The prose dialogue uses commas and quotation marks correctly.#Incorrect. That supports prose quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The poem's random spacing makes the central image harder to read and does not create a clear effect.#Correct. This identifies a format choice that weakens readability.
~D. The reflection uses a paragraph break when time shifts.#Incorrect. That supports clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L04_Q25::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which checklist best matches this lesson's sequence?{
~A. Decorate the page, ignore the genre, remove clarity, skip explanation.#Incorrect. This does not match the lesson. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Choose random spacing, avoid revision, submit without checking.#Incorrect. The lesson requires purposeful quality checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Only count words and ignore format.#Incorrect. Word count alone does not ensure format-specific quality.
=D. Identify the format, decide what the reader needs, revise format features, explain the reader effect.#Correct. This follows the format-specific quality process.
}
